            {
              "id": "133193db-3103-3ff5-a300-d7189e631b40",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a potential buffer overflow vulnerability that can occur when using the `memcpy` function in C++. The `memcpy` function is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. If the size of the destination buffer is smaller than the size of the source buffer, it can lead to a buffer overflow, which can cause a program to crash or, in some cases, allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `memcpy` function is used to copy data from `GFX11_SW_PATTERN_NIBBLE2[pPatInfo->nibble2Idx]` to `&pSwizzle[8]`. If the size of `GFX11_SW_PATTERN_NIBBLE2[pPatInfo->nibble2Idx]` is greater than the size of `&pSwizzle[8]`, it can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied from the source buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the source and destination buffers before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, you should add a check to ensure that the size of `&pSwizzle[8]` is greater than or equal to the size of `GFX11_SW_PATTERN_NIBBLE2[pPatInfo->nibble2Idx]` before calling `memcpy`. If it's not, you should handle the error appropriately, for example by returning an error code or throwing an exception.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to depend on the following libraries:\n\n- The C++ Standard Library, for the `memcpy` function.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "549a465c-f0c6-34d5-9489-95767f298fb2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `memcpy` function in C++. This function is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. The vulnerability arises when the size of the destination buffer is less than the size of the source buffer. This can lead to buffer overflow, which can cause the program to crash or, in worst-case scenarios, allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied from the source buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the size of the destination buffer `pSwizzle[12]` is not checked before calling `memcpy`. This can be fixed by adding a check before the `memcpy` call:\n\n```cpp\nif (sizeof(pSwizzle[12]) >= sizeof(GFX11_SW_PATTERN_NIBBLE3[pPatInfo->nibble3Idx])) {\n    memcpy(&pSwizzle[12],\n           GFX11_SW_PATTERN_NIBBLE3[pPatInfo->nibble3Idx],\n           sizeof(GFX11_SW_PATTERN_NIBBLE3[pPatInfo->nibble3Idx]));\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not provide enough context to determine the exact library dependencies. However, the `memcpy` function is part of the C standard library, so the `<cstring>` header file should be included.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8701a0da-b44b-3499-be19-500fd826fa2a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a potential buffer overflow vulnerability in the `memcpy` function in C++. The `memcpy` function is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to a buffer overflow if the source data is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to a variety of security issues, including arbitrary code execution, data corruption, and application crashes.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source data. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy`, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s`, which include built-in bounds checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the size of the destination buffer `pSwizzle[16]` is not checked before calling `memcpy`. A potential fix could be:\n\n```cpp\nif (sizeof(pSwizzle) >= sizeof(GFX11_SW_PATTERN_NIBBLE4[pPatInfo->nibble4Idx])) {\n    memcpy(&pSwizzle[16],\n           GFX11_SW_PATTERN_NIBBLE4[pPatInfo->nibble4Idx],\n           sizeof(GFX11_SW_PATTERN_NIBBLE4[pPatInfo->nibble4Idx]));\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet appears to depend on the following libraries:\n\n- A standard C library for the `memcpy` function.\n- A custom library or code module where `GFX11_SW_PATTERN_NIBBLE4` and `pPatInfo` are defined.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7199a454-7cd6-3a8a-8bcc-02dfb611c149",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function does not check for the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source is larger than the destination. This is a common vulnerability in C++ programming that can lead to serious security issues such as data corruption, system crash, or even code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the source and destination before calling `memcpy`. Alternatively, you can use functions that do check the size of the destination buffer, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nif (sizeof(m_swizzleModeTable) >= sizeof(SwizzleModeTable)) {\n    memcpy(m_swizzleModeTable, SwizzleModeTable, sizeof(SwizzleModeTable));\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this version, we first check if the size of `m_swizzleModeTable` is larger or equal to the size of `SwizzleModeTable`. If it is, we proceed with the `memcpy`. If it is not, we handle the error in a way that is appropriate for the specific program.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed for this code to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a8b1a4fc-137e-3305-a200-01e0805978ab",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in the memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions, which is not allowed for memcpy and can result in unpredictable behavior. Additionally, there are no explicit checks ensuring that the source and destination are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The destination is a fixed-size array, but the count argument is based on the size of the source, not the destination, which could also lead to buffer overflows if the source is larger than the destination. No guards or capacity checks are present, and the dataflow analysis suggests a feasible overlap scenario. All these factors contribute to the high confidence in this being a real issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 0\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between potentially overlapping regions, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. Additionally, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are not null before performing the copy, and that the size being copied does not exceed the capacity of the destination buffer.\n\n```cpp\nif (m_swizzleModeTable != nullptr && SwizzleModeTable != nullptr) {\n    size_t copy_size = sizeof(m_swizzleModeTable) < sizeof(SwizzleModeTable) ? sizeof(m_swizzleModeTable) : sizeof(SwizzleModeTable);\n    memmove(m_swizzleModeTable, SwizzleModeTable, copy_size);\n}\n```\n\nThis code checks for null pointers and ensures that the copy size does not exceed the destination buffer's capacity, preventing buffer overflows and undefined behavior due to overlapping memory.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## In Context Remediation 0\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between potentially overlapping regions, use a memory-safe copy function that handles overlap, such as `memmove`, instead of `memcpy`. Additionally, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are non-null before performing the copy. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (m_swizzleModeTable != nullptr && SwizzleModeTable != nullptr) {\n    memmove(m_swizzleModeTable, SwizzleModeTable, sizeof(SwizzleModeTable));\n}\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination buffers overlap, and it avoids dereferencing null pointers. Always verify that the size being copied does not exceed the capacity of the destination buffer.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
